**Anagram Checker in Java**
================================

An anagram is a word or phrase formed by rearranging the letters of a different word or phrase, typically using all the original letters exactly once.

Here's a simple Java method to check if two strings are anagrams:

```java
public class AnagramChecker {

    /**
     * Checks if two strings are anagrams.
     *
     * @param str1 the first string
     * @param str2 the second string
     * @return true if the strings are anagrams, false otherwise
     */
    public static boolean areAnagrams(String str1, String str2) {
        // If the strings are not of the same length, they cannot be anagrams
        if (str1.length()!= str2.length()) {
            return false;
        }

        // Convert both strings to lowercase to ignore case differences
        str1 = str1.toLowerCase();
        str2 = str2.toLowerCase();

        // Create an array to store the frequency of each character in the first string
        int[] charFrequency = new int[256]; // ASCII character set size

        // Count the frequency of each character in the first string
        for (char c : str1.toCharArray()) {
            charFrequency[c]++;
        }

        // Decrement the frequency of each character in the second string
        for (char c : str2.toCharArray()) {
            charFrequency[c]--;
        }

        // If all frequencies are zero, the strings are anagrams
        for (int freq : charFrequency) {
            if (freq!= 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(areAnagrams("listen", "silent")); // true
        System.out.println(areAnagrams("hello", "world")); // false
    }
}
```

This code works by first checking if the two strings are of the same length. If not, it immediately returns `false`, as anagrams must have the same number of characters.

Next, it converts both strings to lowercase to ignore case differences.

It then creates an array to store the frequency of each character in the first string. It counts the frequency of each character by iterating over the string and incrementing the corresponding index in the array.

After that, it decrements the frequency of each character in the second string by iterating over the string and decrementing the corresponding index in the array.

